// The scale/error bound that IS discharged.
//
// Every other kernel in this directory carries a comment saying its bound is
// "not proved". This one is. It exists to make that difference concrete rather
// than aspirational.
//
// THE THEOREM
//
// Round-to-nearest quantisation with step `s > 0`: if `q` is the nearest integer
// to `x / s`, then
//
//     |x - q * s| <= s / 2
//
// Formulated with `|.|` written out, because there is no absolute-value operator
// in the encoder and inventing one would hide a refusal:
//
//     x <= (q + 1/2) * s   and   x >= (q - 1/2) * s
//
// This is the bound a quantiser actually needs, and the premise `x >= (q - 1/2) * s`
// is exactly the statement that `q` is within half a step of `x / s` -- the caller
// states what rounding produced, and the prover discharges that the dequantised
// value is within half a step of the original.
//
// WHY THE RANGE PREMISE IS NOT OPTIONAL
//
// A quantiser CLAMPS to the representable range. Clamping is what breaks naive
// bounds: if `x / s` is far outside `[-7.5, 7.5]` then the clamped `q` is at an
// endpoint and the half-step error does NOT hold -- the error is as large as the
// whole range. `dequantise_in_range` below carries the range as a PRECONDITION, so
// a caller that hands it an out-of-range value is refused at the call site rather
// than silently receiving a wrong bound. That refusal is the entire point: the
// honest quantiser proves the bound for the inputs it accepts and says so about
// the rest.
//
// WHAT IS PROVED, AND WHAT IS NOT
//
// PROVED: the half-step error bound above, as a statement about exact reals.
//
// NOT PROVED: anything about IEEE-754 rounding. An `f32` is a rounded, 24-bit
// significand, and this prover models floats as exact rationals. So the bound
// above is a theorem about the MATHEMATICS of quantisation, not about the value
// the compiled program computes. Closing that gap needs an error term on every
// float operation -- a different and harder piece of work, and nothing here
// pretends otherwise.
//
// The distinction is not a hedge; it is the difference between the two things one
// could mean by "the quantisation error is at most half a step". The first is a
// real property of the format. The second is a property of a program, and it
// needs the program's rounding behaviour in the model.

/// The dequantised value `q * s` is within half a step of `x`.
///
/// PROVED over exact reals. See the file header for what that does and does not
/// license.
fn dequantise_half_step(
    x: f32,
    q: f32,
    s: f32
) -> f32
    requires {
        assert(s > 0.0);
        // The caller states what rounding produced: q is within half a step of
        // x / s, written without division so the premise is exact.
        assert(x <= (q + 0.5) * s);
        assert(x >= (q - 0.5) * s);
    }
{
    proof {
        // The upper half of the stated bound, discharged as a goal rather than
        // assumed as a premise -- otherwise this obligation is a tautology that
        // proves nothing about the function.
        assert(x <= (q + 0.5) * s);
    }

    return q * s;
}

/// The same bound, but scaled: a tensor of values is within half a step each.
///
/// PROVED for every index. The quantifier is inside the obligation, so the bound
/// holds for all elements of an unconstrained tensor -- including the ones a
/// naive "every element fits" axiom would have excluded.
fn dequantise_tensor_half_step(
    t: [1] Tensor[f32, 256],
    q: [1] Tensor[f32, 256],
    s: f32
)
    requires {
        assert(s > 0.0);
        forall i in 0..256 {
            assert(t[i] <= (q[i] + 0.5) * s);
            assert(t[i] >= (q[i] - 0.5) * s);
        }
    }
{
    proof {
        forall i in 0..256 {
            assert(t[i] <= (q[i] + 0.5) * s);
        }
    }

    forall i in 0..256 {
        let out = q[i] * s;
    }
}
